chx:C_62 a sen0414:PrimarySourceConcept ; rdfs:label "route"@en ; sen0414:conceptKind "Concept" ; dcterms:source chx:P01 .
chx:C_63 a sen0414:PrimarySourceConcept ; rdfs:label "invoice"@en ; sen0414:conceptKind "Concept" ; dcterms:source chx:P01 .
chx:C_64 a sen0414:PrimarySourceConcept ; rdfs:label "onion routing"@en ; sen0414:conceptKind "Concept" ; dcterms:source chx:P01 .
chx:C_65 a sen0414:PrimarySourceConcept ; rdfs:label "Sphinx"@en ; sen0414:conceptKind "Concept" ; dcterms:source chx:P01 .
chx:C_66 a sen0414:PrimarySourceConcept ; rdfs:label "BOLT"@en ; sen0414:conceptKind "Concept" ; dcterms:source chx:P01 .
chx:C_67 a sen0414:PrimarySourceConcept ; rdfs:label "pathfinding"@en ; sen0414:conceptKind "Concept" ; dcterms:source chx:P01 .
chx:C_68 a sen0414:PrimarySourceConcept ; rdfs:label "fee"@en ; sen0414:conceptKind "Concept" ; dcterms:source chx:P01 .
chx:C_69 a sen0414:PrimarySourceConcept ; rdfs:label "capacity"@en ; sen0414:conceptKind "Concept" ; dcterms:source chx:P01 .
chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's list of building blocks, 18 guarantees from no double spend to predictable issuance, was run where it can be run: on a private chain of Bitcoin Core 31.1 a second spend of an output paying 50,000 satoshis was refused as an insufficient replacement, one paying 200,000 replaced the first, which paid 100,000, after a block the loser was refused as spending a missing or spent input, a transaction with outputs above its inputs was refused with bad-txns-in-belowout, and a payment signed at height 301 was still accepted at height 451, so no double spend, accounting and nonexpiration hold as the book says, with the note that replacement of an unsignalled transaction is now the default (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P77 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The two applications that the chapter derives from the building blocks were built: the SHA-256 of a document was written in an OP_RETURN output and read back from a confirmed block, and a Lighthouse-style crowdfunding transaction of two pledges that met a goal of 2 coins was refused with one pledge, accepted with both and refused when its output was changed after signing, because ANYONECANPAY signatures cover only their own input and the outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lau and Wuille, 2016)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P32 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The three mechanisms of colored coins were rebuilt by hand: three outputs served as single-use seals each closed by the next transaction, a pay-to-contract key equal to the plain key plus the contract hash times the generator point was spendable with the tweaked key and not with the plain one, and a checker found all three steps of a 69-byte-contract history equal to the commitments on the chain and a forged contract not; the idea of the seal is described by Peter Todd in December 2017 and the overview of colored coins by Meni Rosenfeld in December 2012, and the tweak is the basis of taproot (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Todd, 2017); (Rosenfeld, 2012); (Wuille et al., 2020)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P28, chx:P27, chx:P33 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The one-way channel of Emma and Fabian is arithmetic and two contracts: 0.01 millibit is 1,000 satoshis a second, an hour is 3,600,000, and the funding output is a 71-byte 2-of-2 script that one signature could not spend; six commitments with lock times from 488 down to 483 were all non-final at the start, only the sixth was valid at height 483 and mining it removed the input of the others, as the book's ladder of lock times predicts, and the refund timelock of 30 days is 4,320 blocks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Decker and Wattenhofer, 2015); (Todd, 2014)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P26, chx:P29 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The revocable commitment of Hitesh and Irene was built with a delayed output of 1000 blocks that was refused at 999 confirmations as non-BIP68-final and accepted at 1000, and a penalty branch that one half of the revocation key could not spend and both halves could, with the key derived as the book says and as BOLT 3 specifies, and the 48-bit state index allows 2 to the power 48, 281,474,976,710,656 states (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Friedenbach et al., 2015); (BtcDrak et al., 2015); (Lightning developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P30, chx:P31, chx:P13 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The hash time lock contract worked in both branches on the test chain: the right 32-byte secret with the receiver's key paid, a wrong secret and the payer's key with the right secret were refused, the refund was non-final one block before the lock height 1514 and valid at it, and the 20-byte hash in the script was the RIPEMD-160 of the 32-byte SHA-256 payment hash, a construction that Tier Nolan discussed for atomic transfers in 2013 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Nolan, 2013); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P60, chx:P64, chx:P41 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The Alice-to-Eric payment of the book was made on five lnd 0.21.3-beta nodes on a private chain with channels of 4 bitcoin and a fee of 0.001 per intermediary: the payment of 1 bitcoin cost 300,000 satoshis, the hops carried 100,200,000, 100,100,000 and 100,000,000 satoshis, the balances changed by -100,300,000, +100,000 three times and +100,000,000 and the sum of the changes was zero, as in the book's 0.997 and 3.003 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lightning developers, 2026); (Lightning Labs, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P13, chx:P72 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A hold invoice made the book's simultaneous HTLCs visible: with 0.1 bitcoin in flight each of the five nodes had a pending HTLC, of 10,300,000, 10,200,000, 10,100,000 and 10,000,000 satoshis along the route, the settlement of the secret completed the payment and the cancellation of a second invoice restored all balances exactly, a mechanism that Optech describes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P41 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The BOLT 8 handshake was recomputed in pure Python from the published test keys: the initiator produced the 50-byte act one, the 66-byte act three and the sending key 969ab31b of the vector, and the 5-byte hello became the 39 bytes of the vector, with the Noise framework as the family of designs (Lightning developers, 2026); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P18, chx:P70 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The BOLT 4 onion of five hops was rebuilt from the vector's session key, node keys and payloads and equalled the 1366-byte packet of the file byte for byte, the packet size was the same for one, two and five hops, and the first hop's HMAC check passed the packet and failed it with one flipped bit, which is the Sphinx design the book names (Lightning developers, 2026); (Danezis and Goldberg, 2009); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P14, chx:P15, chx:P25, chx:P01 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The invoices of the book are bech32 strings of BOLT 11: the coffee example of the specification decoded as 2500 micro-bitcoin, 250,000,000 millisatoshi, with a 60-second expiry and a 32-byte payment hash, the invoice of the test payment decoded as 1 bitcoin with the payment hash, the payment secret and the final delta of 80 that lnd reported, and the released secret hashed to the payment hash (Lightning developers, 2026); (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P20, chx:P40 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The key derivations of BOLT 3 were recomputed: the local key, the revocation public key and the revocation private key of appendix E equalled the vector, and the per-commitment secret of the highest index for a seed of all ff bytes equalled the published one, so the book's half-secrets are in the specification as two weighted terms (Lightning developers, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P13, chx:P01 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The penalty rule was shown on two lnd nodes: with a channel of 10 bitcoin moved from 5 and 5 to 7 and 3, the restored node of Irene broadcast the revoked commitment, a transaction of 279 virtual bytes with four outputs, and Hitesh's node answered with a justice transaction of two inputs that brought his wallet 999,990,380 satoshis, and listed the channel as a breach close, which is what BOLT 5 calls the ugly way of closing (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lightning developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P16 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A channel is two transactions on the chain: the funding output of 4 bitcoin was a witness script hash with the two keys in lexicographic order and the cooperative close, of 193 virtual bytes with lock time 0 and two taproot outputs worth 399,995,175 satoshis, was valid at once, while the newer simple taproot channels of lnd use MuSig2 in the funding output (Lightning developers, 2026); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P13, chx:P50 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Fee bumping of commitments changed with Bitcoin Core 28.0: a version-3 parent of 123 virtual bytes with a zero-value pay-to-anchor output and no fee was refused alone with min relay fee not met and accepted with a child of 151 virtual bytes through submitpackage at an effective rate of about 10.9 satoshis per virtual byte, as the package rules, BIP 431 and BIP 433 describe, and as the ephemeral-anchor topic explains (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Zhao, 2024); (Sanders, 2025); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P77, chx:P76, chx:P38, chx:P39, chx:P54 .
chx:F16 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Replacement changed from opt-in to the rule: BIP 125 defines opt-in signalling, release 28.0 changed the default of mempoolfullrbf from 0 to 1, the option is gone from the help of 31.1, and on the test chain the higher-fee second spend replaced the first (Harding and Todd, 2015); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P37, chx:P77, chx:P78 .
chx:F17 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The Lightning Network is no longer proposed: the statistics page of mempool.space counted 32,518 channels, 16,225 nodes and 375,370,668,018 satoshis, about 3,753.7 bitcoin, on 30 August 2026, an average of 4.0 channels per node, and Wikipedia says that Lightning Labs launched the network in 2018 (Mempool Space, 2026); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P79, chx:P65, chx:P01 .
chx:F18 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Erratum of the source file: the AsciiDoc of chapter 14 begins with the anchor [[ch12]] while chapter 12 begins with [[mining]], and the errata appendix of the book, which is about the whitepaper, does not mention it, as the same kind of left-over was found in the file of chapter 13 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P09, chx:P07 .
chx:F19 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The date of the first description holds: the book says February 2015 and Wikipedia cites a copy of version 0.5 archived on 28 February 2015, while the paper kept at lightning.network is the draft 0.5.9.2 of 14 January 2016, 320 days later, with a short summary document beside it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026); (Poon and Dryja, 2016); (Poon and Dryja, 2015)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P65, chx:P23, chx:P24 .
chx:F20 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The book's numbers are teaching sizes: its revocable delay is 1,000 blocks and its HTLC timeouts step by one block from 10 to 7, while lnd channels reported a delay of 2016 blocks, a reserve of one percent and expiries that step by 80, with 80 as the default delta in the sample configuration (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lightning developers, 2026); (Lightning Labs, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P12, chx:P72 .
chx:F21 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The book's fee of 0.001 per hop, 0.3 percent for three hops, is far from the median of the public network, a base fee of 500 millisatoshi and 100 parts per million, which gives about 30,001 satoshis for 1 bitcoin over three hops, 0.03 percent, while the averages, 904 millisatoshi and 823 parts per million, are higher than the medians (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Mempool Space, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P79 .
chx:F22 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The script of the book writes HASH160 of H, while BOLT 3 writes the RIPEMD-160 of the SHA-256 payment hash for the 20-byte comparison; the two are the same computation on the same secret, and the invoice's payment hash stays the 32-byte SHA-256 so that one secret serves all hops (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lightning developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P13 .
chx:F23 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The book's five implementation teams are in the saved sources as Core Lightning, Eclair and the Lightning Development Kit besides lnd 0.21.3-beta, the only one run; the READMEs describe them and the release notes of lnd record its changes, but interoperability was not tested (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Core Lightning developers, 2026); (ACINQ, 2026); (Spiral, 2026); (Lightning Labs, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P81, chx:P82, chx:P83, chx:P71 .
chx:F24 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Some of the improvements that designers of channels want are not in Bitcoin: BIP 118 for an eltoo-style update, BIP 119 for templates, and the topology rules of BIP 431 and the anchor of BIP 433 carry the status Draft in the saved files, and Optech lists eltoo as a proposed mechanism, so statements that rely on them are plans (Decker and Towns, 2017); (Rubin, 2020); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P35, chx:P36, chx:P52 .
chx:F25 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The story of the pizzas has three dates in three sources: the forum thread shows 18 May 2010 for the offer, the Bitcoin Wiki says 17 May, which can only be a matter of the clock used, and Wikipedia dates the purchase to 22 May 2010, when a 19-year-old in California named Jeremy Sturdivant ordered two pizzas for the 10,000 bitcoins (Hanyecz, 2010); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P63, chx:P61, chx:P68 .
chx:F26 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The families of payment channels in the Bitcoin Wiki differ in what they need: the Spillman style of 2013 is one-way, the Decker and Wattenhofer duplex channels need the relative lock time of BIP 68, and the Poon and Dryja channels have an indefinite life and are bidirectional, which is the design the BOLTs specify (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Decker and Wattenhofer, 2015); (Poon and Dryja, 2016)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P56, chx:P26, chx:P23 .
chx:F27 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Several parts of the Lightning ecosystem are not in the chapter: watchtowers that act when a node is offline, splicing, channel factories, multipath payments and the offers of BOLT 12 are described by Optech and the BOLTs and were not run here, and are listed with their reasons among the ten items that the sandbox could not execute (Bitcoin Optech, 2026); (Lightning developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P45, chx:P46, chx:P43, chx:P44, chx:P48, chx:P21 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "69 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "83 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-15T19:00:23Z; 54 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
